159
00:15:20,100 --> 00:15:22,060
Okay, so let's get started.

160
00:15:22,440 --> 00:15:26,900
And to start things off, I think what we need to do is consider a definition.

161
00:15:26,900 --> 00:15:33,020
I'm going to define what love is, but then most of the experiments I'm going to talk

162
00:15:33,020 --> 00:15:36,860
about are really focused more on attraction than love.

163
00:15:37,320 --> 00:15:42,580
And I'm going to pick a definition from a former colleague, Robert Sternberg, who is

164
00:15:42,580 --> 00:15:48,880
now the dean at Tufts University, but was here on our faculty at Yale for nearly 30

165
00:15:48,880 --> 00:15:54,480
years. And he has a theory of love that argues that it's made up of three

166
00:15:54,480 --> 00:16:01,960
components, intimacy, passion and commitment or what is sometimes called decision

167
00:16:01,960 --> 00:16:05,440
commitment. And these are relatively straightforward.

168
00:16:06,360 --> 00:16:11,700
He argued that you don't have love if you don't have all three of these elements.

169
00:16:12,720 --> 00:16:18,140
Intimacy is the feeling of closeness, of connectedness with someone, of bonding.

170
00:16:19,400 --> 00:16:23,720
Operationally, you could think of intimacy as you share secrets, you share

171
00:16:23,720 --> 00:16:27,360
information with this person that you don't share with anybody else.

172
00:16:28,080 --> 00:16:33,560
Okay, that's really what intimacy is, the bond that comes from sharing information

173
00:16:33,560 --> 00:16:35,880
that isn't shared with other people.

174
00:16:37,100 --> 00:16:39,120
The second element is passion.

175
00:16:40,280 --> 00:16:42,540
Passion is the drive that leads to romance.

176
00:16:43,240 --> 00:16:45,480
You can think of as physical attraction.

177
00:16:45,480 --> 00:16:51,280
And Sternberg argues that this is a required component of a love relationship.

178
00:16:53,220 --> 00:16:57,560
The third element of love in Sternberg's theory is what he calls decision

179
00:16:57,560 --> 00:17:02,020
commitment, the decision that one is in a love relationship,

180
00:17:02,720 --> 00:17:07,520
the willingness to label it as such and a commitment to maintain that relationship,

181
00:17:07,600 --> 00:17:09,900
at least for some period of time.

182
00:17:10,720 --> 00:17:15,920
Sternberg would argue it's not love if you don't call it love and if you don't have

183
00:17:15,920 --> 00:17:18,120
some desire to maintain the relationship.

184
00:17:19,500 --> 00:17:25,300
So if you have all three of these, intimacy, passion and commitment, in Sternberg's

185
00:17:25,300 --> 00:17:26,480
theory, you have love.

186
00:17:27,580 --> 00:17:32,740
Now, what's interesting about the theory is what do you have if you only have one

187
00:17:32,740 --> 00:17:35,000
out of three or two out of three?

188
00:17:35,660 --> 00:17:40,140
What do you have and how is it different if you have a different two out of three?

189
00:17:41,220 --> 00:17:45,980
What's interesting about this kind of theorizing is it gives rise to many different

190
00:17:45,980 --> 00:17:50,780
combinations that can be quite interesting when you break them down and start to

191
00:17:50,780 --> 00:17:51,780
look at them carefully.

192
00:17:52,420 --> 00:17:57,760
So what I've done is I've taken Sternberg's three elements of love, intimacy,

193
00:17:57,960 --> 00:18:03,160
passion and commitment, and I've listed out the different kinds of relationships you

194
00:18:03,160 --> 00:18:09,720
would have if you had one, two or three out of the three elements.

195
00:18:36,420 --> 00:18:41,880
Question 17. What does Robert Sternberg argue about love?

196
00:18:54,780 --> 00:19:01,640
Question 18. What question does a speaker think is interesting about Sternberg's

